Description: Enlarge an output live: output zoom and the zoom command
 People with low vision, and anyone presenting their screen, need the
 part of the desktop they work in shown larger without freezing it.
 Raising an output's scale resizes every window, tells clients the
 output changed and cannot follow the pointer; screenshot magnifiers
 show a still image.
 .
 New settings:
   output <name>|* zoom <factor>|off       factor 1 to 32 (1 = off)
   zoom in|out [<step>]                     step > 0, default 1
   zoom set <factor> | zoom off | zoom toggle
   zoom_mouse_tracking centered|proportional|push|none  (proportional)
   zoom_focus_tracking centered|push|none                (none)
 The zoom command acts on the output under the pointer and behaves like
 output <name> zoom; toggle restores that output's last factor above 1,
 or 2. A refused value (out of range, nan, inf, a step of 0 or less, an
 unknown mode) changes nothing.
 .
 The scene of an enlarged output is rendered into a private swapchain
 the size of the output, then the shown rectangle of it (the view,
 width/factor by height/factor, kept inside the output) is drawn
 enlarged into the output's buffer, so output scale and transform keep
 working. The view follows the pointer and, other than through the
 pointer, keyboard focus. Windows, outputs and input coordinates are
 not changed, so clients see nothing; a click acts on what the picture
 shows under the pointer, and screencopy records the enlarged picture.
 Software cursors are drawn enlarged with everything else, and direct
 scan-out is off while an output is enlarged, since a client buffer
 put straight on screen would show the layout unenlarged.
 .
 get_outputs reports a zoom object (factor, view in layout coordinates,
 both tracking modes) for every active output; sway(5), sway-output(5)
 and sway-ipc(7) document it all.
 .
 Not forwarded yet: upstream would rather build this on an output
 viewport in the wlroots scene
 (https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/wlroots/wlroots/-/issues/4019).
